WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.480
So, last week we visited the cork tree factory,
and also the harvesting.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.720
You could see how the bark is taken off,
 and processed in a factory into cork.

00:00:09.626 --> 00:00:12.944
And, we actually used to have
three bigger cork trees on the land ourselves!

00:00:12.944 --> 00:00:16.880
This one right here, still standing near the bottom yoga deck.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.200
This is the only one that didn't burn down...

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.280
And, we used to have two other ones.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.120
Here's another cork tree underneath my feet!

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.200
I'm walking on it right now,
because it collapsed a few months ago.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.880
Makes you realize how big these things are, 
so that you can just really walk on top of them.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.160
But, here, you can see actually how it went.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.880
So, it got hit by a fire, it got burned,
but kept being alive.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.484
But, then, some windy day, 
eventually the whole thing did crack and collapse...

00:00:46.088 --> 00:00:49.560
Which is a shame, because it was a very 
beautiful tree, close to Basekamp.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.040
And, the third cork tree was actually located right here.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.800
But, there isn't a tree anymore, because we actually used it.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.200
And, this tree also was burned down, 
and fell on the neighbors land.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.000
He dumped it on our land, so we could use it.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.480
But, it was there just rotting away...

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.003
So, last winter, we started to process it.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.640
In this video, I'm going to show you what we did with it.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.040
All the cool things we made.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.640
And, we actually also are going to bake
some bread in our clay-made pizza oven.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.400
So, yeah, welcome to new Project Kamp update!

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.323
Let's rewind!

00:01:22.817 --> 00:01:25.337
The tree has actually already been here for a while.

00:01:25.337 --> 00:01:28.680
And, with winter coming,
it slowly starts to rot away even more...

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.600
So, it's a good time to chop it.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.120
We're going to make some little stools,
and a little table out of it, hopefully.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.131
And, we could actually borrow João's chainsaw, which is super long!

00:01:40.131 --> 00:01:42.200
So, we can really cut through
the thick parts.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:43.300
Let's start.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.200
So, here we have a nice selection of wood.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.720
However, I did notice
a lot of them already started to rot...

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:14.400
Like here, it's got some mold on it,
some animals living in there...

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:21.960
So, we might want to scrape off the outer edges, 
and make sure they don't rot anymore.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.484
Here's the biggest part of the tree, and it's quite big!

00:02:39.484 --> 00:02:43.960
Like, even this big chainsaw, as you can see,
it won't reach all the way.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.736
So, here we can probably make a nice table out of it.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.321
These two parts might look smaller...

00:02:53.321 --> 00:02:58.384
But, they also come together here,
 creating probably the biggest piece of it all!

00:03:00.058 --> 00:03:04.341
Yeah, let's see, if we can chop them down 
with that small chainsaw...

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:30.200
It took quite a while to go through this old dense wood...

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.200
But, we managed to cut out a few good pieces.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.440
It's a rough cut and still needs some cleanup, 
but lifting them up is impossible.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.760
Way too heavy!

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.480
So, when our neighbor drove by,
with his front loader on this tractor...

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.760
We asked for some help, but they were actually also
 too heavy for lifting with his tractor.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.160
So, we had another creative way!

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.880
And, we dragged out all the pieces
that we thought would be somewhat useful.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.880
Including this burned up piece, as a potential bench.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.918
And, here's the heaviest one of them all!

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:46.760
We wanted to put them up for a while,
so they could dry up from that winter humidity they've been in.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.120
And, just as a reference, to show how big they are, 
here's one Spanish guy next to a cork.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.000
And, as you can see,
the cork still has some bark around it.

00:05:06.357 --> 00:05:08.573
If you want to know about what you can do with that...

00:05:08.573 --> 00:05:12.837
Have a look at our previous video, where we visit a place,
where they harvest and process cork!

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.789
But, the cork isn't that usable anymore...

00:05:15.789 --> 00:05:20.440
Since the bark already started to decompose,
and became a resource for another species...

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.648
Many mushrooms and worms in there.

00:05:23.648 --> 00:05:25.720
It feels bad to take away their house.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:29.120
But, it also feels bad, seeing all this valuable cork wood rot away.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.614
It could still be used for many years!

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:36.440
So, after dragging all the pieces into Basekamp, 
we let them dry for a while.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.240
And after that, it's ready for cutting.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.958
First up, a simple table from this big chunk.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.240
Next up, this massive block.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:49.860
And, the first step was to take off the bark...

00:05:50.107 --> 00:05:54.485
And, looking at the tape measure,
it seems like actually two tables are hidden in there.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:56.635
But, we can't cut it like this...

00:05:56.635 --> 00:05:59.354
So, yeah, I guess we just flip it over and cut.

00:05:59.354 --> 00:06:00.435
Easy...

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.840
And, finally, this rough one, like a bench sort of...

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.160
Well, with a few simple cuts,
it could probably be very useful.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.280
So, after all of this, 
we also had a few small pieces laying around.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:30.794
We cut those up as a little pathway,
to keep your feet clean after a shower.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:35.440
At this point, we're just looking for applications, 
to make sure to cork doesn't rot away.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.280
So, we cut a bunch of stuff...

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.160
Now, it's time to actually install it all in Basekamp.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.400
Let me show you how we are
using them right now, a few months later.

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:47.160
So, this is the final result.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.481
Now, we have a super cool bench!

00:07:49.865 --> 00:07:53.145
This bench was chopped down, as you might have seen.

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:56.200
This was a bit sanded and oiled.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:03.720
In front, we have this little, living table,
with some of big mushrooms here.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:07.160
We can work a bit, drink some tea, whatever...

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:10.240
Just being in a space under the shade.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:13.760
But even better, there's this space right here.

00:08:19.283 --> 00:08:22.280
It's really under the shade, and it's perfect for meetings.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:29.680
And this again, was also super sanded and treated,
like with some linseed oil.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:33.600
So, that it resists a bit better to the weather.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.200
And, Jerram created a few beautiful gardens here!

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:45.160
We expect in a few years that this is all covered up, 
with jasmine or some climbing plants.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.255
And, besides all of these places...

00:08:48.722 --> 00:08:55.502
Tim also created another super beautiful space 
in this direction, which is right there!

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:10.080
So, you guys might not really feel it, 
but the temperature is super different.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.640
Here, there is some breeze and it's under the shade.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:16.800
This is just the ideal space to have some meetings!

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.760
Even better than on the deck,
because here the shade is from the trees.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:23.160
So, it really cools down the area.

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:29.960
This is one of the bigger parts of the cork tree.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.360
Also treated with oil, and sanded.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:40.709
Some hammocks just to enjoy the siestas a bit, 
when the heat goes up.

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.760
Tim has making some experiments with mimosa.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:50.400
And, one of the experiments was this pull-up bar.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.400
I personally wouldn't be able to do it and I wouldn't do it.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.520
But, this is a super cool addition also to the land!

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:06.240
And, with the leftover bits,
 we created benches all around the Kamp.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:07.840
This one is a nice one.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.840
Just next to the kitchen with a little heart.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:19.702
Also, we chopped a few thinner bits,
that serve as steps we can go up.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.160
Or, we can go down much easier.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.120
Just follow the path up through the shower.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:33.320
That actually leads us to the space
 that we will clean up today.

00:10:35.233 --> 00:10:38.760
Yeah, the cork bench is there, 
or the cork table is there...

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:44.830
We will just clear all of this, put some wood chips,
and it's ready to go.

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:49.801
So, this section will be made by Coleen and I.

00:10:49.801 --> 00:10:52.800
Coleen just arrived, and she’s a great addition to the team!

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:56.451
- Hello, I'm Coleen, and I'm here for two months!

00:10:56.451 --> 00:11:01.426
- And, today, we're cutting some bushes,
and removing some nasty spikes.

00:11:01.646 --> 00:11:03.288
Yes, we are!

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:20.560
Coleen, what are we doing now?

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:22.640
- So, we cleared up the area.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:24.360
That means that the soil is very exposed...

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:27.720
Because, we took out of the soil and brambles.

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:29.597
So we're going to put wood chips on it.

00:12:29.597 --> 00:12:35.240
And, it will allow the soil to be more conserved,
to not be too much in the heat...

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:40.200
And, also to have a comfortable mattress,
for our little feet, when we're resting.

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.200
- Oh, yeah!

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:40.960
So, the space is done!

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:42.320
It's super cool!

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:47.920
Now, this would be very shaded during the day,
and it will be super nice to hang out here.

00:13:49.622 --> 00:13:53.520
Now, we took brambles away,
and we replaced it with woodchips.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:56.200
So, much better for the feet.

00:13:57.243 --> 00:14:02.840
We also took out a lot of big mimosas, 
and at the moment, they're stacked on a big pile.

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:07.920
This pile is going to get chopped in pieces,
 and put under the oven.

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:12.352
So, then, when we're going to do the bread,
we're going to use that wood.

00:14:13.480 --> 00:14:18.720
And, now, we are off, because we're going to 
watch a movie without you!

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:23.280
So, we should go! Bye, bye!

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.244
And, another ongoing project we have here,
 is our research on breadmaking.

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:37.280
Making a bread yourself every now and then isn't too hard.

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:41.826
But, we're trying to find a way,
to realistically integrate it in our weekly routine!

00:14:41.826 --> 00:14:45.160
Like, baking community amounts,
and using a wood-fired clay oven.

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:47.000
Yeah, it's quite a mission...

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:49.080
So, we have an ongoing research topic in our module.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:53.880
Where you can find more information about it,
 starting with burnt bread, and gradually improving.

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:56.760
Let me show you what the current
state of our baking process is.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:00.000
- Hello, who are you?

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.131
Hello! Hey Felix!

00:15:01.131 --> 00:15:06.560
I'm Ted, I'm from Netherlands, and I’m the vegan chef,
 here at Project Kamp for about two months.

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:09.440
And, today, we're going to make sourdough bread.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:13.172
We'll start with the first step,
and in the first step we'll be making a levain.

00:15:13.172 --> 00:15:15.302
And, we'll explain to you what a levain is.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.720
- So, you came after Agi, right?

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:19.391
Yes, I came after Agi.

00:15:20.187 --> 00:15:22.043
- Okay, cool. Let's start!

00:15:22.482 --> 00:15:24.615
Well, this is the starter for the sourdough.

00:15:24.615 --> 00:15:27.560
So, that's a culture you keep on feeding.

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:30.120
It becomes wild yeast.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:31.560
This starter is called Bert.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.121
So, it has a name,
and you have to take care of it.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.560
And, we're going to take two scoops of the starter

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:42.331
We're going to mix it with equal parts of 
flour and water to make the levain.

00:15:42.331 --> 00:15:45.000
Which will be the kickstarter, for making the sourdough bread.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:48.720
So, that's going to make sure the sourdough bread
 is actually going to ferment.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:55.320
So, it doesn't come super precise, 
but we take about two scoops of the sourdough starter.

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:04.680
We measured our amount of flour, 
because we want equal parts of flour and water.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:08.560
So, it's going to be a 100% hydration in your levain.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:12.120
And, after that, you're going to play 
with hydration in your dough.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:14.240
But, with the levain, you want 100% hydration.

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:15.960
So, equal parts, water and flour.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:27.401
Just getting tap water.

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:35.400
- How long are you going to wait
till you make the dough out of it?

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:38.360
So, I'm going to let this sit overnight.

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.200
It's going to ferment all the way.

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.221
It's going to double in size.

00:16:43.017 --> 00:16:49.495
So, it's going to are going to rise and ferment all night,
 and tomorrow morning, we can use it.

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:57.080
- So, we have here the starter already fed the levain?

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.720
And, later or tomorrow, we're going to do the dough?

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:02.840
- Yes, that's it!

00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:05.600
So, Bert is going to live inside the fridge again.

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:08.920
We don't need to feed it constantly,
so we're going to let it go to sleep.

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:15.963
So, the fermentation and the yeast is less active in the starter,
so it's going to go back into the fridge.

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:22.431
So we covered the levain with the wet cloths

00:17:22.431 --> 00:17:31.193
To keep it moist inside the bowl,
to make sure it doesn't dehydrate during the fermentation process.

00:17:32.181 --> 00:17:33.880
Then, we see it tomorrow again...

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:36.000
- See you tomorrow!

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:38.880
So, it's the next morning

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.160
And, we're now have all the components
to make our sourdough.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:46.080
So, first, we have the levain, 
that we already made yesterday.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:49.760
This fermented overnight and it became a lively culture!

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:53.423
Next to that, we have our flour of course.

00:17:53.423 --> 00:17:57.400
It contains 80% of whole wheat flour and 20% white flour.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:01.080
We have water, and we have salt.

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:04.840
We're going to mix all these ingredients together, 
and make one cohesive dough.

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:07.584
So, let's do it!

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:10.000
Okay, so, we start with the levain.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:11.400
You mix that in first.

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:12.634
So, we're going to mix in the levain.

00:18:12.634 --> 00:18:16.417
And after that, the water, 
to spread the the yeast throughout the water.

00:18:16.829 --> 00:18:20.311
To make sure it's spread out in the dough, 
in a later stage as well.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:26.000
So, now, we pour in the water.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:36.000
We give it a good stir.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:39.000
So, that the levain mixes in with the water.

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:49.200
We can mix in the flour and salt.

00:18:54.964 --> 00:18:56.720
- How much flour is that?

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:58.920
This is two and a half kilos of flour.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.920
So, we'll make four loaves!

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:09.240
And, it's about 50 grams of salt, 
so 2% of the weight of the flour.

00:19:34.360 --> 00:19:36.180
- How long is it going to rest?

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:41.000
It’s going to rest for 4 hours.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.824
I mean, normally, I would say 6 or 8 hours...

00:19:43.824 --> 00:19:49.080
But now, I think 4 to 5 hours with these temperatures,
because it's 30 degrees plus here.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:52.080
So, I would say 4 to 5 hours.

00:20:23.314 --> 00:20:26.680
Now, the dough has risen, so it's like fluffy.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:29.160
You can push it a little bit and bounces back.

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:32.347
So, we know it's ready.

00:20:40.496 --> 00:20:42.000
- Full of air!

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:58.491
We roughly shape the dough, 
but we make them in a baking tray.

00:20:58.491 --> 00:21:00.159
So, they don't need to be shaped that much.

00:21:00.159 --> 00:21:03.960
But, you do want to build up a little bit of tension...

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:11.360
On the top part of the of the dough, 
so it will rise better once it's in the oven.

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:26.639
Ready to be baked!

00:21:48.995 --> 00:21:49.960
- Add some water?

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:52.960
Yeah, some water to create some steam.

00:21:52.960 --> 00:21:58.727
So, they don't directly seal, 
so the crust doesn't directly form.

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:04.251
- How long do they bake?

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:09.720
They bake about 45 minutes normally, but often...

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:15.240
You have to look a little bit, and see how they develop...

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:17.280
Take them out, to see whether they are good...

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:21.440
So, 45 minutes at least, I would say, 
but maybe up to an hour.

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:57.835
- You can hear it from here!

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:07.756
Bread dinner! I like it!

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:36.000
All right, so, that was it for this video!

00:23:36.549 --> 00:23:37.669
Thank you guys for watching!

00:23:37.669 --> 00:23:40.640
If you already want to see the next video,
make sure to support on Patreon!

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:43.800
Or not, and we'll see you again next week.

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:45.794
Same time, same channel! Bye!

